WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.017
Randomly masking and predicting tokens has powered
pretraining in language modeling.

00:00:06.097 --> 00:00:07.417
This paper, Uni-MASK,

00:00:07.497 --> 00:00:11.948
shows the same idea applies naturally to sequential
decision making.

00:00:12.028 --> 00:00:16.879
Many well-studied tasks, behavior cloning, offline
reinforcement learning,

00:00:16.959 --> 00:00:19.811
inverse dynamics, and waypoint conditioning,

00:00:19.891 --> 00:00:25.475
all correspond to different maskings over a
sequence of states, actions, and returns.

00:00:25.555 --> 00:00:28.208
Uni-MASK unifies them into one framework,

00:00:28.288 --> 00:00:34.205
and a single model trained this way often matches
or beats specialized single-task models,

00:00:34.285 --> 00:00:37.670
and consistently outperforms them after fine-tuning.

00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:41.854
In sequential decision making, tasks like behavior
cloning,

00:00:41.934 --> 00:00:45.079
offline reinforcement learning, inverse dynamics,

00:00:45.159 --> 00:00:50.015
and goal or waypoint conditioning are typically
each handled by a separate,

00:00:50.095 --> 00:00:51.595
specially trained model.

00:00:51.675 --> 00:00:56.927
Yet all of these tasks operate over the very same
object: a trajectory of states,

00:00:57.007 --> 00:00:58.309
actions, and returns.

00:00:58.389 --> 00:01:05.089
Training a distinct model per task ignores this
shared structure and misses the chance to build richer,

00:01:05.169 --> 00:01:07.590
reusable representations across tasks.

00:01:07.970 --> 00:01:11.222
Masked language modeling, the technique behind BERT,

00:01:11.302 --> 00:01:15.194
trains models to predict randomly masked tokens in
a sequence,

00:01:15.274 --> 00:01:19.871
producing rich bidirectional representations that
transfer to many tasks.

00:01:19.951 --> 00:01:24.356
The authors observe that this idea maps directly
onto decision making.

00:01:24.436 --> 00:01:28.649
If you treat states and actions as tokens and mask
the last action,

00:01:28.729 --> 00:01:32.109
predicting it is exactly a behavior cloning inference.

00:01:32.189 --> 00:01:37.171
Different tasks are simply different masking
patterns over the same trajectory,

00:01:37.251 --> 00:01:42.040
so a single masked-prediction objective can, in
principle, express them all.

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:45.542
The main contribution is the Uni-MASK framework,

00:01:45.622 --> 00:01:51.413
a unified way to specify models for sequential
decision making by casting each inference

00:01:51.493 --> 00:01:54.948
task as a masking scheme over a trajectory of states,

00:01:55.028 --> 00:01:57.150
actions, and reward-to-go tokens.

00:01:57.230 --> 00:01:59.284
Because tasks are just maskings,

00:01:59.364 --> 00:02:04.554
a single model can be trained to perform behavior
cloning, reward conditioning,

00:02:04.634 --> 00:02:08.690
dynamics modeling, and goal or waypoint
conditioning together.

00:02:08.770 --> 00:02:14.761
The authors show this single model often matches or
exceeds specialized single-task models,

00:02:14.841 --> 00:02:18.230
and consistently outperforms them after fine-tuning.

00:02:18.310 --> 00:02:23.500
Along the way they also introduce Decision-GPT, an
improved GPT-based baseline.

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:28.929
Uni-MASK represents a trajectory as a sequence of
per-timestep tokens: a state,

00:02:29.009 --> 00:02:33.085
an action, and optionally a property token such as
return-to-go,

00:02:33.165 --> 00:02:34.773
the sum of future rewards.

00:02:34.853 --> 00:02:40.162
A masking scheme specifies two things: which input
tokens are visible to the model,

00:02:40.242 --> 00:02:44.317
and which output tokens the model must predict and
be scored on.

00:02:44.397 --> 00:02:47.044
Different schemes recover different tasks.

00:02:47.124 --> 00:02:52.498
Behavior cloning conditions on past states and
actions and predicts the next action;

00:02:52.578 --> 00:02:56.004
goal conditioning additionally reveals a future state;

00:02:56.084 --> 00:02:58.666
reward conditioning reveals return-to-go.

00:02:58.746 --> 00:03:05.224
The model itself is a bidirectional BERT-style
transformer encoder that stacks each timestep's state,

00:03:05.304 --> 00:03:09.639
action, and property into one vector and predicts
the masked tokens.

00:03:09.719 --> 00:03:13.210
The authors compare four training regimes: single-task,

00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:17.495
multi-task with a random scheme per snippet, fully
random masking,

00:03:17.575 --> 00:03:21.780
and random-mask pretraining followed by
task-specific fine-tuning.

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:25.200
The framework is evaluated on two environments.

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:26.661
The first is MiniGrid,

00:03:26.741 --> 00:03:31.440
a gridworld where an agent must reach a fixed goal
behind a locked door;

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:37.481
it is used to qualitatively demonstrate the many
inference tasks a single model can perform

00:03:37.561 --> 00:03:40.601
and to compare task-specific validation losses.

00:03:40.681 --> 00:03:46.244
The second is Maze2D, a continuous-control maze
from the MuJoCo-based D4RL benchmark,

00:03:46.324 --> 00:03:52.086
where the authors measure test-time reward over one
thousand rollouts across five seeds,

00:03:52.166 --> 00:03:56.799
comparing Uni-MASK against a feedforward network,
Decision Transformer,

00:03:56.879 --> 00:04:02.110
and their own improved Decision-GPT baseline at
context lengths of five and ten.

00:04:02.490 --> 00:04:04.186
In the MiniGrid environment,

00:04:04.266 --> 00:04:09.703
a single model trained with random masking
outperforms single-task models on all tasks,

00:04:09.783 --> 00:04:16.361
and adding task-specific fine-tuning on top of
random-mask pretraining gives the best performance of all,

00:04:16.441 --> 00:04:20.483
beating single-task models on every task except
behavior cloning.

00:04:20.563 --> 00:04:24.478
This means that even if you only care about one
inference task,

00:04:24.558 --> 00:04:27.332
first training on many tasks generally helps.

00:04:27.412 --> 00:04:29.424
In the harder Maze2D environment,

00:04:29.504 --> 00:04:35.639
fine-tuning becomes critical: the fine-tuned
Uni-MASK models reach rewards around two point seven,

00:04:35.719 --> 00:04:38.936
outperforming every baseline at context length five,

00:04:39.016 --> 00:04:44.580
including a Decision Transformer that scores only
around one point one to one point five.

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:49.723
The training-regime comparison isolates the effect
of each ingredient and shows fine-tuning

00:04:49.803 --> 00:04:54.565
is the decisive one for good performance in the
more complex Maze2D environment.

00:04:54.645 --> 00:04:59.407
A second,

00:04:59.487 --> 00:05:04.250
controlled comparison pits single-task Uni-MASK
against the Decision-GPT baseline,

00:05:04.330 --> 00:05:09.092
where the only real difference is a BERT-style
versus a GPT-style backbone.

00:05:09.172 --> 00:05:13.935
Here the picture is nuanced: BERT works well at a
short context length of five,

00:05:14.015 --> 00:05:18.777
but at length ten the BERT-style Uni-MASK models
degrade and are outbeaten by the GPT-based Decision-GPT,

00:05:18.857 --> 00:05:23.620
revealing a known difficulty of BERT-like
architectures with longer-sequence generation.

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:26.112
A few numbers capture the impact.

00:05:26.192 --> 00:05:28.304
In Maze2D at context length five,

00:05:28.384 --> 00:05:34.216
the fine-tuned Uni-MASK models reach reward around
two point seven three on both behavior

00:05:34.296 --> 00:05:36.342
cloning and reward conditioning,

00:05:36.422 --> 00:05:42.320
compared to just one point one three and one point
four nine for the Decision Transformer,

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:47.036
and around one point five to one point seven for a
feedforward network.

00:05:47.116 --> 00:05:51.620
These results are averaged over five seeds and one
thousand rollouts.

00:05:51.700 --> 00:05:55.937
And critically, a single Uni-MASK model handles
behavior cloning,

00:05:56.017 --> 00:05:58.528
reward conditioning, dynamics modeling,

00:05:58.608 --> 00:06:01.650
and goal and waypoint conditioning all at once.

00:06:02.030 --> 00:06:07.518
The lasting takeaway is simple and powerful: many
seemingly distinct sequential-decision

00:06:07.598 --> 00:06:11.125
tasks are just different maskings of the same trajectory,

00:06:11.205 --> 00:06:15.808
so a single masked-prediction model can replace a
zoo of specialized ones.

00:06:15.888 --> 00:06:19.288
And training on many masking schemes, then fine-tuning,

00:06:19.368 --> 00:06:23.148
generally does better than training on any single
task alone,

00:06:23.228 --> 00:06:25.932
even when you only care about that one task.

00:06:26.012 --> 00:06:28.020
The main caveat is architectural,

00:06:28.100 --> 00:06:33.652
BERT-style models shine at short contexts but
struggle to generate over longer sequences,

00:06:33.732 --> 00:06:39.410
suggesting that combining GPT-style backbones with
random masking is a promising next step.
